  Why needed: To confirm that the absence of this scenario in some system instances is not a requirements violation, but an intentional part of the system's abstraction.

=== COUNTEREXAMPLE ANALYSIS ===
Scenario: The JSON describes a trace in which the system transitions from Normal to Emergency mode (State$0 → State$2), with three requests (Request$0, Request$1, Request$2) all submitted by User$0 and all targeting Object$0. In Emergency mode (State$2), only Request$0 is processed, with the rest remaining pending. However, all requests have the same classification (determined by Object$0's classification), so the required scenario of "at least one pending request at each classification (High, Medium, Low)" in Emergency mode does not occur—the model never realizes the scenario the assertion expects.
What went wrong: The assertion expects the existence of a state in Emergency mode with at least one pending request at each classification, but the model permits system instances where all requests are at the same classification, making the scenario impossible and the assertion violated.
Root cause: Ambiguous/overly strong requirement—the assertion encodes a scenario that is only required to be possible under explicit enabling assumptions (as now clarified by requirements), not universally; its universal check is thus invalid.
Fix: Restrict the assertion and corresponding scenario predicate to only those model runs in which explicit enabling assumptions guarantee the presence of at least one pending request at each classification in Emergency mode; exclude from universal verification runs, or refactor the assertion to only apply in such scenarios.
